WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.360
The largest AI models today can have over a trillion parameters, which would take something

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:12.040
like two terabytes of memory to store. But the largest GPU on the market holds about

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:18.480
288GB, which is nowhere near enough to store these huge frontier models.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:24.079
So how are we running these models behind the chatbots everyone uses reliably for millions of

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:30.400
people? The truth is, there is no single mega computer hosting the AI models you talk to every

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:36.560
day. They're running on a coordinated system of dozens, sometimes hundreds of GPUs.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:43.240
Models need to be stored, run and support real user traffic in production. Confining this whole

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:48.720
process to one GPU quickly introduces real constraints, bottlenecks and latency to your

00:00:48.759 --> 00:00:54.080
system. So let's fix this with distributed inference. The process of running an AI model by

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.320
splitting the computational load across multiple interconnected devices or servers. A model is

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:06.489
composed of layers, all storing "weights", or the knowledge of the model. To run a model, all the

00:01:06.489 --> 00:01:13.410
weights need to be initially stored in memory. On top of that, as the model runs, it accrues

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:18.689
what we call working memory. Additional information about the conversation that allows it

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:25.369
to remember what was previously said. This context, which we call the KV cache, is held in memory as

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:30.929
well on top of the model itself. And lastly, every request takes a set amount of time to move

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:37.009
through a GPU. If there are too many requests coming in at once, they get backed up in a queue

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:42.650
and every user waits longer for a response. If we're hosting this for real users, we might have

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:47.289
hundreds or thousands of people trying to reach the model at once. So we need to be able to

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:53.209
concurrently serve many users. Serving a model in production means solving these three constraints

00:01:53.209 --> 00:01:58.449
at the same time. Model memory footprint. Whether the weights physically fit on the hardware, you

00:01:58.449 --> 00:02:04.179
have, KV cache growth, the content that keeps growing while you're serving and requests

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:10.299
throughput, how many concurrent users you can serve. So let's start small. Let's say our model

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:16.899
and its KV cache fits fine on one GPU, but we still have too many users to serve them all at

00:02:16.899 --> 00:02:23.539
once. What we can do is copy the entire model onto multiple GPUs, and then route

00:02:23.539 --> 00:02:29.499
request to them intelligently based on things like how loaded each GPU currently is, or which

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:36.419
replica already has relevant cached context. This is data parallelism. Every replica is a full

00:02:36.419 --> 00:02:40.699
identical copy of the model. There's no coordination needed between them. You're just

00:02:40.699 --> 00:02:46.139
deciding which door each request walks through. But realistically, many of the smartest models

00:02:46.139 --> 00:02:53.020
can't fit on a single GPU. Recall that a model is really just a series of layers holding individual

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:58.499
knowledge. Running inference means passing your input through these layers one at a time, kind of

00:02:58.539 --> 00:03:05.149
like an assembly line. So if our whole model is too big to fit, we can split or

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:12.070
shard the model by layers. Say layers one and two live on the first GPU, layers three and four live

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:18.910
on the second and so on. Real models will have a lot more layers, but you guys get the idea. This is

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:25.389
pipeline parallelism. Every GPU holds only certain layers of the model that can comfortably fit in

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:30.669
its memory. Now, if we send only one request through at a time, most of the GPUs sit idle,

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:37.070
waiting for their turn to take full advantage of pipeline parallelism. Think assembly line. Keep

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:42.389
requests continuously flowing through so every station stays busy instead of waiting on the one

00:03:42.389 --> 00:03:48.229
before it. We split the model vertically Now let's look at splitting it horizontally. Tensor

00:03:48.229 --> 00:03:54.749
parallelism divides each individual layer itself rather than dividing the model by layers. Every

00:03:54.750 --> 00:04:00.949
GPU gets a slice of the same layer, computes its piece of the layer's math, and then communicates

00:04:00.949 --> 00:04:06.160
with the others to combine their partial results back together. They then repeat that process for

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:12.999
every single layer. That combining step is called a collective operation and its blocking for

00:04:12.999 --> 00:04:19.639
each layer because the GPUs have to constantly communicate mid computation. This only works when

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:25.799
they're connected by a high bandwidth, low latency GPU interconnect, typically sitting inside the

00:04:25.799 --> 00:04:31.279
same server. Sending that chatter across a slower network could actually cause us to become a

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:38.079
bottleneck rather than an optimization. There's also a more specialized case, a type of model

00:04:38.079 --> 00:04:45.079
called a mixture of experts or MoE model. This model, instead

00:04:45.079 --> 00:04:51.039
of holding one block of weights that runs for every single input, holds smaller subnetworks or

00:04:51.079 --> 00:04:56.639
experts that are meant to specialize in different parts of response generation. One could lean

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.488
towards code samples. One could do punctuation. Maybe one does numbers

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:09.249
because you've got so many subnetworks bundled together. MoE models tend to be humongous overall.

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:16.648
Expert parallelism spreads those experts across GPUs, so they each hold a slice of experts instead

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:23.569
of a copy of the entire model. At every MoE layer, each token gets dispatched to the handful

00:05:23.569 --> 00:05:30.249
of experts its router picked. Modern models will pick about eight experts out of 256,

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:37.170
and then it'll all be combined back. This means far less computation per token, but paid for

00:05:37.209 --> 00:05:42.929
in all this GPU to GPU network traffic of dispatching the individual tokens and then

00:05:42.929 --> 00:05:48.849
combining the information afterward. Let's look at one last optimization. Prefilled decode

00:05:48.849 --> 00:05:55.609
disaggregation. During inference, you'll go through two main stages. Prefill

00:05:56.769 --> 00:06:03.738
and decode. And Prefill is

00:06:03.739 --> 00:06:10.179
where the model reads and understands your input. It operates on a fixed set of model weights and

00:06:10.179 --> 00:06:17.019
requires a lot of parallel computations at once. Its compute-bound, meaning the speed of this

00:06:17.019 --> 00:06:23.779
step is determined by how fast it can perform that math. This step builds the KV cache, our bank

00:06:23.779 --> 00:06:30.539
of working memory with each token it processes. Decode is where the model builds its

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:37.339
response to the user. It generates a reply one token at a time, and it has to

00:06:37.379 --> 00:06:44.339
pull the entire KV cache for every step. Unlike these models weights, the KV cache is not fixed,

00:06:44.339 --> 00:06:50.858
so the demand for memory grows with every step of decode. Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound,

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:56.539
meaning its speed is determined by how fast the data can be transferred from the memory. If

00:06:56.540 --> 00:07:01.900
co-located the decode phase's, increasing demand for memory can start to eat into the prefilled

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:07.790
stage's territory as this KV cache grows. Because these two phases stress completely different

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:11.789
parts of the hardware, it's actually more efficient to split them onto their own separate

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:18.589
pools of GPUs, each one individually optimized for its own bottleneck. That said, this

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:25.230
split only pays off if this handoff itself is super fast. The whole KV cache has to

00:07:25.269 --> 00:07:30.670
move from the prefill pool to the decode pool. So if the connection between the two pools is too

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:36.989
slow, you'll burn through the time you just saved. In practice, that means the two pools need to sit

00:07:37.029 --> 00:07:42.629
on dedicated, low latency networking designed for high throughput data movement between compute

00:07:42.629 --> 00:07:49.149
nodes, not just a regular network link. Plain TCP over standard Ethernet usually isn't fast enough

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:54.670
to make this split worth it, so traffic gets solved with more replicas. Too much model gets

00:07:54.670 --> 00:08:00.630
solved by splitting the weights by layer, by tensor, or by expert and too much growing memory

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:05.839
doing two different kinds of work gets solved by giving each phase its own dedicated pool of

00:08:05.839 --> 00:08:12.199
hardware. In production, real deployments layer multiple techniques together at once. Tensor

00:08:12.199 --> 00:08:19.160
parallelism within a server. Pipeline parallelism across servers. Data parallelism across replicas.

00:08:19.399 --> 00:08:24.999
Expert parallelism for mixture of expert models and disaggregation, splitting the serving pipeline

00:08:24.999 --> 00:08:31.999
by phase. This is called multi-dimensional parallelism. And on top of it

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.919
all sits an orchestration layer. Routing every request to the right pool of GPUs. Balancing

00:08:38.919 --> 00:08:44.959
load and handling hardware failures without dropping requests. This is a super complex topic,

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.799
so drop any follow up questions in the comments or let me know how you're optimizing your model

00:08:49.799 --> 00:08:52.039
deployments with distributed inference.

